"""ORD-0408 ruling A, part two: the three arms as three files, and the mutants that prove they bite.

What was missing from the 03:4x delivery was not the readings - it was the 反控. Twelve arms that all
pass say nothing about whether any of them could fail, so this run mutates the ruler at the six
decision points ruling A actually rests on and requires that a NAMED arm flips for each mutation. A
mutant nobody catches is reported and the script exits non-zero: that is the difference between a
ruler with teeth and a ruler that agrees with itself.

Mutants, each one a defect this lineage has actually had today:
  M1 union_fallback           `contractSetFor` falls back to every enum in the document
                              (the draft I removed after B7/B9 measured it)
  M2 enum_key_owns_the_set    the `enum:` key line becomes the property name
                              (the first parse, which reported CONTRACT_MISSING_ENUM on a contract
                              that did declare the set)
  M3 silent_contract_is_pass  a contract naming no set exonerates anyway
  M4 ignore_the_chain         set membership is never checked against the value's own literals
  M5 refused_cell_may_fold    a cell the contract refused is handed to the disclosure fold
  M6 exempt_by_field_name     ruling B restored: naming a `*_code` field is permission enough

Arm files, one per arm as the order asks:
  arm_before_tip_contract.json  the LIVE contract at the tip, which names no set for `reason_code`
  arm_after_contract_with_enum.json the same cell with a contract that does name one, inline and block
  arm_mutant_r7b4.json          the six mutants above, with the arm each one flips
"""
